# mariadb_inst — real-world examples

## Homelab: install on OpenSUSE Tumbleweed

```sh
sudo zypper install mariadb mariadb-client
sudo systemctl enable --now mariadb
sudo mysql_secure_installation
mysql -u root -p
```

## Install on CentOS/RHEL 7 from the MariaDB YUM repo

```sh
# Add the MariaDB YUM repo file
sudo vim /etc/yum.repos.d/MariaDB.repo
```

```ini
# CentOS 7
[mariadb]
name = MariaDB
baseurl = http://yum.mariadb.org/10.1/centos7-amd64
gpgkey=https://yum.mariadb.org/RPM-GPG-KEY-MariaDB
gpgcheck=1

# RHEL 7: same stanza with
# baseurl = http://yum.mariadb.org/10.1/rhel7-amd64
```

```sh
yum install MariaDB-server MariaDB-client -y
systemctl start mariadb
systemctl enable mariadb
systemctl status mariadb
```

## Secure a fresh install

```sh
mysql_secure_installation
# Remove anonymous users: y
# Disallow root login remotely: y
# Remove test database: y
# Reload privilege tables now: y
```

## Log in and verify

```sh
mysql -V                   # client version
mysqld --print-defaults    # effective server defaults
mysql -u root -p
```

## Homelab: confirm it survives a reboot

```sh
sudo systemctl is-enabled mariadb
sudo journalctl -u mariadb --since today
```

